<section id="how">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>How I Work</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="how-intro tbd">Whether it’s a warehouse, a showroom, or a website, I tend to work the same way.</p>
    <div class="principles">
      <div>
        <h3>Learn how it really works</h3>
        <p class="tbd">Before I change anything, I watch how the work actually moves, not just how it’s supposed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Make it make sense to everyone</h3>
        <p class="tbd">If a process only works for the person who built it, it isn’t finished. I write it down and keep it simple enough for anyone to pick up.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Leave it better than I found it</h3>
        <p class="tbd">I build things to keep working without me: clear steps, clear owners, and fewer surprises.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="example">
      <h3>In practice</h3>
      <p class="tbd">At a multi-location distribution company, I researched and selected a digital proof-of-delivery system to replace paper records. I mapped how it would affect every role from drivers to accounting, worked out problems before launch, and led the team through the switch. Accounting went from five or six people to three, and nobody lost their job.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="how-note">I later shaped this approach into a framework I call the Seven Pillars. You can see it in the <a href="#made">Bay Circle project</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

